        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using pure whey isolate for soft baking</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Isolate firms up fast in the oven and is the usual cause of dry, rubbery protein muffins. For pancakes, muffins and quick breads, a whey and casein blend holds more moisture. Swap only a quarter to a third of the flour, add yogurt or banana, and take the tray out early.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Dumping powder into hot coffee</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Whey clumps when it hits near-boiling liquid, which is where most proffee complaints come from. Let the coffee cool a few minutes, mix the powder into a little cold milk first, then stir it in, or use a frother. Iced coffee with a fast-dissolving isolate is the easiest route.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stacking sweeteners in smoothies</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ripe banana, mango, dates, sweetened milk and a sucralose vanilla together can make a smoothie cloying. If your smoothies already lean sweet, pick a milder vanilla such as a monk fruit or stevia powder, or use half vanilla and half unflavored protein to keep the fruit in charge.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the sweetener you already dislike</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Vanilla exposes sweeteners more than chocolate does, so a stevia finish or sucralose aftertaste you barely notice in chocolate can dominate a vanilla. Check the ingredient list before you buy, and if you are unsure, start with a small tub or sample pack rather than five pounds.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing two-scoop and one-scoop servings directly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some vanilla powders reach their protein figure with a two-scoop serving, others with one. Compare protein per serving and cost per 25 g of protein, not tub price, and divide protein by serving weight to see how much of the powder is protein rather than sweetener and flavor.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

